      "writtenOn": "2024-11-14T19:16:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is curious to me why we are checking if multiplying these two values is finite instead of checking them individually. Would this result in us failing to invert something that could be inverted? Same for the below check. We really want something equivalent to `std::isfinite(invSX) \u0026\u0026 std::isfinite(invSY)`, right?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 17,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "39d7babc2d1f1fb3bf056437991691257f727260",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7a6332da_ed102ff5",
        "filename": "src/core/SkMatrix.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 825,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-14T19:57:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`SkIsFinite(...)` is faster than `std::isfinite()` at evaluating many floats that all have to be finite, but is equivalent \u0026\u0026\u0027ing `std::isfinite()` calls together.\n\nThe multiply that it is doing internally is effectively an \u0026\u0026 operation because it will always stay 0 or become NaN, which then fails the final `prod \u003d\u003d prod` check.\n\nHowever, if you\u0027re wondering why we are checking `invSX` and `invSY` or `invTX` and `invTY`, vs. checking the original matrix\u0027s scale and translate components for finiteness, it\u0027s to make sure that the resulting inverse matrix remains finite, since there are cases where the original matrix is finite but calculating the reciprocal scale factor or dividing the translation component by the scale stop being finite.",
      "parentUuid": "e80bc21c_4b02c6c3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 825,
        "startChar": 17,
        "endLine": 825,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "39d7babc2d1f1fb3bf056437991691257f727260",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
